---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'shortcut', label: 'The local shortcut that hides bugs' },
  { id: 'start', label: 'Start both services with a seeded secret' },
  { id: 'exercise', label: 'Rotate a secret and encrypt a payload' },
  { id: 'paths', label: 'The paths you can now test' },
];
const secSeed = `services:
  secretmanager:
    secrets:
      - name: stripe-webhook-secret
        versions:
          - data: fake-local-webhook-secret-v1`;
const secStart = String.raw`localcloud start --local-only --services secretmanager,kms
eval "$(localcloud env)"

curl -fsS -X POST "$LOCALCLOUD_GATEWAY/seed" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/yaml' \
  --data-binary @secrets-seed.yaml`;
const secPy = `import os
import grpc
from google.cloud import kms, secretmanager
from google.cloud.kms_v1.services.key_management_service.transports import KeyManagementServiceGrpcTransport
from google.cloud.secretmanager_v1.services.secret_manager_service.transports import SecretManagerServiceGrpcTransport

project = os.environ["GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT"]
secrets = secretmanager.SecretManagerServiceClient(
    transport=SecretManagerServiceGrpcTransport(
        channel=grpc.insecure_channel(os.environ["SECRET_MANAGER_EMULATOR_HOST"])
    )
)
keys = kms.KeyManagementServiceClient(
    transport=KeyManagementServiceGrpcTransport(
        channel=grpc.insecure_channel(os.environ["KMS_EMULATOR_HOST"])
    )
)

# Resolve "latest", rotate, and resolve again.
secret = f"projects/{project}/secrets/stripe-webhook-secret"
print(secrets.access_secret_version(name=f"{secret}/versions/latest").payload.data.decode())
secrets.add_secret_version(parent=secret, payload={"data": b"fake-local-webhook-secret-v2"})
print(secrets.access_secret_version(name=f"{secret}/versions/latest").payload.data.decode())

# Envelope step: encrypt and decrypt with a local KMS key.
location = f"projects/{project}/locations/global"
ring = keys.create_key_ring(parent=location, key_ring_id="app", key_ring={})
key = keys.create_crypto_key(
    parent=ring.name,
    crypto_key_id="payload-key",
    crypto_key={"purpose": kms.CryptoKey.CryptoKeyPurpose.ENCRYPT_DECRYPT},
)
ciphertext = keys.encrypt(name=key.name, plaintext=b"card-token-123").ciphertext
print(keys.decrypt(name=key.name, ciphertext=ciphertext).plaintext.decode())`;
const output = `fake-local-webhook-secret-v1
fake-local-webhook-secret-v2
card-token-123`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="local-secrets-kms-without-iam-friction"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Production reads its secrets from Secret Manager and encrypts with Cloud KMS. Local development reads a .env file. The code in between only ever runs after you deploy. Run both services on localhost and delete the shortcut."
  primary={{ href: '/services/secret-manager/', label: 'Secret Manager guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/services/cloud-kms/', label: 'Cloud KMS guide' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A seeded secret you rotate through the real Secret Manager client, and a payload encrypted and decrypted with a local Cloud KMS key."
  needs="Python with google-cloud-secret-manager and google-cloud-kms"
  next={{ posts: ['one-second-cloud-state-resets', 'zero-secret-gcp-ci-github-actions'], doc: { href: '/services/cloud-kms/', label: 'Cloud KMS service guide: key purposes and algorithms' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="shortcut">The local shortcut that hides bugs</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Somewhere in most cloud codebases is a branch like <code>if os.getenv("ENV") == "local"</code> that skips Secret Manager and reads an environment variable. It exists because giving every laptop key rings and IAM roles was more work than anyone wanted.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The cost shows up later. Version lookup, the missing-secret error path and the encrypt and decrypt wrappers are the code that runs in production, and none of it runs on the machine where it was written. A typo in a resource name becomes a deploy-time discovery.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="start">Start both services with a seeded secret</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud serves <a href="/services/secret-manager/">Secret Manager</a> and <a href="/services/cloud-kms/">Cloud KMS</a> from its gateway. Seed a synthetic secret so the application finds it at startup.</p>
    <BlogCode label="secrets-seed.yaml (synthetic values only)" code={secSeed} copy="copy-blog-sec-seed" />
    <BlogCode label="Start and seed" code={secStart} copy="copy-blog-sec-start" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="exercise">Rotate a secret and encrypt a payload</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Point the standard clients at the local endpoints and use them exactly as production does. The only local detail is the plaintext channel; put it behind the one function that builds your clients.</p>
    <BlogCode label="secrets_check.py" code={secPy} copy="copy-blog-sec-python" />
    <BlogCode label="Output" code={output} />
    <p class="mt-4">The first two lines are <code>latest</code> before and after a rotation. The third is a round trip through a KMS key that exists only on your machine.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="paths">The paths you can now test</h2>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Rotation.</strong> Add a version, disable the old one, and check that the application picks up the new value without a restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing secrets.</strong> Ask for a secret that doesn't exist and the client raises <code>NotFound</code>, the same exception your error handling must catch in production.</li>
      <li><strong>Envelope encryption.</strong> Encrypt a data key with KMS, store the ciphertext, and decrypt it on the way back, with symmetric or asymmetric keys.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean starts.</strong> Keep the seed file in the repository so a new clone boots with every secret the service expects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">Seed synthetic values only, and browse what is stored in the <a href="/docs/console/">web console</a>. IAM policy and HSM protection are properties of your Google Cloud project, so check those there.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
